<h2>OEE formula</h2><p>Overall Equipment Effectiveness is commonly calculated as <strong>Availability × Performance × Quality</strong>. Availability is runtime divided by planned production time. Performance compares ideal-cycle output with runtime. Quality is good count divided by total count.</p>
<h2>Loss categories behind OEE</h2><p>The three factors help separate time lost to stops, speed/small-stop losses and quality losses. OEE can also simplify algebraically to good count × ideal cycle time ÷ planned production time when definitions are consistent, but retaining the three components makes diagnosis easier.</p>
<h2>Why performance can exceed 100%</h2><p>Performance above 100% usually means the “ideal” cycle time is not truly the fastest sustainable design/reference cycle for that product, the unit count is inconsistent, or runtime boundaries differ. Do not simply cap the value and hide the data-quality issue.</p>
<h2>Avoid universal OEE targets</h2><p>Benchmarks such as 85% are widely cited for some discrete-manufacturing contexts, but there is no universal ideal number for every process. Product mix, asset type and business constraints differ. Use consistent internal loss definitions and improve the loss categories that matter.</p>
<h2>Make OEE loss data actionable</h2><p>OEE becomes more useful when every stop, speed loss and quality loss is coded consistently enough to build a Pareto of causes. Report the three factors with OEE so a plant can see whether a change came from availability, rate or quality. Comparing lines with different products is meaningful only when ideal-cycle standards and planned-time rules are equally rigorous; otherwise the benchmark can reward looser standards.</p>
